# Test: a subscriber that runs no IPv4 Protocol-Rejects IPCP, and ze stops sending it.
# QUESTION:  when the peer answers ze's IPCP Configure-Request with an LCP
#            Protocol-Reject naming 0x8021, on ze's IPv4-only pool where IPCP is
#            the only NCP ze runs, does ze stop sending IPCP, never bring the
#            session up, and end it at the no-NCP bound through the NAS Error
#            path, with no LCP Terminate-Request?
# VALIDATES: spec-ppp-ncp-independent-lifetime AC-2 in its IPCP form (the
#            rejected NCP takes the Down event and ze sends no further IPCP
#            packet) and AC-12 (no NCP Opened when the NCP phase settles: no
#            session-up, and at the no-NCP bound the session ends through
#            ze's fail path with NAS Error and no LCP Terminate-Request). User
#            story 2. RFC 1661 Section 5.7: "Upon reception of a
#            Protocol-Reject, the implementation MUST stop sending packets of
#            the indicated protocol at the earliest opportunity."
# PREVENTS:  a Protocol-Reject naming an NCP being read only as the LCP
#            automaton's RXJ+, which leaves IPCP retransmitting until its
#            Max-Configure is spent; a session with no NCP brought up; and a
#            session with no NCP held for ever.
#
# Why not a unit test: TestProtocolRejectIPv6CPKeepsIPv4Session and its IPCP
# sibling drive the PPP session over a fake channel. Only the daemon joins a
# real proxy-LCP call, kernel PPPoL2TP, the configured `lcp restart-timer` and
# `ncp timeout`, and the CDN on the L2TP wire, so only a .ci shows the
# subscriber's side of the exchange.
#
# The peer answers the FIRST IPCP packet ze sends with the Protocol-Reject and
# fails on any IPCP packet after it, naming its Code and Identifier. It ends
# when ze's CDN arrives, which must name the session, carry Result Code 2 and
# follow no LCP Terminate-Request: the Idle Timeout end that follows session-up
# sends one, the NCP-phase fail does not.
#
# The one horizon is the window between the Protocol-Reject and the CDN. An IPCP
# packet that does NOT come proves something only over a span in which an
# unfixed ze would have sent one, so the peer refuses a CDN that came within one
# Restart time of the Protocol-Reject. `lcp restart-timer 2000` is that Restart
# time, which IPCP retransmits at, and `ncp timeout 6` the bound, three Restart
# times after the NCP phase began: a ze that ignored the Protocol-Reject sends
# its second IPCP Configure-Request about 2s in, well before the CDN. The peer's
# second argument repeats 2000, because the peer cannot read ze's config.
#
# The NAS Error cause is not on the L2TP wire: the CDN Result Code is 2 for
# every PPP failure. It is read from ze's log instead, under the daemon: the
# NCP-phase bound fails the session with "ncp: timeout after 6s with no NCP
# opened", and that fail path is the one whose session-down carries NAS Error
# (pppSession.fail, internal/component/l2tp/ppp/session_run.go). The reject of
# "l2tp: PPP session up" under the daemon reads the daemon's whole span, so no
# session-up was logged at any point of the run. That line's positive control
# is test/l2tp/ppp-ncp-terminate-after-up.ci, which expects it under the same
# logging.
#
# Revert walk: remove the `if s.receiveProtocolReject(pkt) { return true }`
# dispatch in handleLCPPacket (internal/component/l2tp/ppp/session_run.go).
# The Protocol-Reject then reaches only the LCP automaton as RXJ+, IPCP keeps
# retransmitting each Restart time, and the peer fails on ze's next IPCP
# Configure-Request about 2s after the Protocol-Reject.
#
# The session negotiates no IPv4, so it needs no address, but the call still
# runs kernel PPPoL2TP, which needs CAP_NET_ADMIN: this test is needs-linux and
# runs in QEMU as root. It SKIPS on an unprivileged dev host.
# ze.l2tp.skip-kernel-probe is deliberately NOT set, as in
# test/l2tp/radius-acct-wire.ci, whose LAC half and RADIUS mock this test
# reuses.
#
# Ports: $PORT is the L2TP listener, $PORT2 the RADIUS mock that accepts the
# call's CHAP credential.

stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
l2tp {
    enabled true
    shared-secret s3cr3t
    auth-method chap-md5
    lcp {
        restart-timer 2000
    }
    ncp {
        timeout 6
    }
    auth {
        radius {
            nas-identifier lns1
            timeout 5
            retries 2
            server mock {
                address 127.0.0.1
                port $PORT2
                accounting-port $PORT2
                shared-key testing123
            }
        }
    }
    pool {
        ipv4 {
            gateway 10.99.32.1
            start 10.99.32.10
            end 10.99.32.10
        }
    }
}
environment {
    l2tp {
        server main {
            ip 127.0.0.1
            port $PORT
        }
    }
}
EOF_CONF

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp:value=debug
option=env:var=TEST_SECRET:value=s3cr3t

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test fixture l2tp/ppp-ncp-protocol-reject-ipcp/radius $PORT2 testing123
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=config
expect=stderr:pattern=ppp: session failed.*ncp: timeout after 6s with no NCP opened
reject=stderr:contains=l2tp: PPP session up
# 90s bounds the peer's wait for the CDN, 70% of this budget, well past the
# 6s bound, so a stall ends with the peer's own message. The happy path takes
# about 8s.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=le test fixture l2tp/ppp-ncp-protocol-reject-ipcp $PORT 2000:timeout=90s:exit=0
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=OK: CHAP Success received
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: LCP Protocol-Reject naming 0x8021 sent for ze's IPCP Code 1, Identifier [0-9]+
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: ze sent no IPCP packet in the [0-9.]+m?s between the Protocol-Reject and its CDN, longer than one Restart time of 2s
expect=stdout:pattern=OK: ze ended session [0-9]+ with a CDN carrying Result Code 2, and sent no LCP Terminate-Request
